The assertion says: "dblclick handler references captured 'li' variable after calling render() — must re-query the DOM instead"

Let me look at the conformance check more carefully:

```js
Then('the dblclick handler does not use the captured {string} variable after calling {string}', function (varName, fnName) {
  const source = this.appSource;
  const dblclickMatch = source.match(/addEventListener\(['"]dblclick['"][^)]*\)\s*\{([^}]*)\}/);
  if (dblclickMatch) {
    const handlerBody = dblclickMatch[1];
    if (handlerBody.includes('render()') && (handlerBody.includes('li.') || handlerBody.includes('li['))) {
      assert.fail(`dblclick handler references captured "${varName}" variable after calling render() — must re-query the DOM instead`);
    }
  }
});
```

The regex is: `/addEventListener\(['"]dblclick['"][^)]*\)\s*\{([^}]*)\}/`

This matches `addEventListener('dblclick'` followed by any chars except `)` then `)` then optional whitespace then `{` then captures any chars except `}` inside `{}`.

My current code:
```js
li.querySelector('label').addEventListener('dblclick', function () {
    todo.editing = true;
    render();
    var editInput = todoList.querySelector('li.editing .edit');
    if (editInput) {
      editInput.focus();
      editInput.selectionStart = editInput.selectionEnd = editInput.value.length;
