```

The regex matches `addEventListener('dblclick', function () {` and then `([^}]*)\}` captures `\n        todo.editing = true;\n        render();\n        var editInput = todoList.querySelector('li.editing .edit');\n        if (editInput) {\n          editInput.focus();\n          editInput.selectionStart = editInput.selectionEnd = editInput.value.length;\n        }\n      ` - wait, the regex `[^}]*` matches any chars except `}`. So it would match `\n        todo.editing = true;\n        render();\n        var editInput = todoList.querySelector('li.editing .edit');\n        if (editInput) {\n          editInput.focus();\n          editInput.selectionStart = editInput.selectionEnd = editInput.value.length;\n        }\n      ` - no, wait. `[^}]*` is greedy and matches any char except `}`. So it would match up to the first `}`. The first `}` is after `editInput.focus();`... wait, there's no `}` before the `if (editInput) {` - the `{` after `if (editInput)` is an opening brace, not a closing brace. So `[^}]*` would match through the `if (editInput) {` line and stop at the `}` that closes the `if` block.

Actually, let me trace through the captured content:
```
\n        todo.editing = true;\n        render();\n        var editInput = todoList.querySelector('li.editing .edit');\n        if (editInput) {\n          editInput.focus();\n          editInput.selectionStart = editInput.selectionEnd = editInput.value.length;\n        }\n      
```

The `[^}]*` matches up to the first `}` character. The characters in the body are:
- `\n        todo.editing = true;\n        render();\n        var editInput = todoList.querySelector('li.editing .edit');\n        if (editInput) {\n          editInput.focus();\n          editInput.selectionStart = editInput.selectionEnd = editInput.value.length;\n        }\n      `

The first `}` is the one closing the `if` block: `}` after `editInput.value.length;`. So the captured group matches everything up to and including that `}`.

So the captured handler body is:
```
\n        todo.editing = true;\n        render();\n        var editInput = todoList.querySelector('li.editing .edit');\n        if (editInput) {\n          editInput.focus();\n          editInput.selectionStart = editInput.selectionEnd = editInput.value.length;\n        }
```

Now the check: `handlerBody.includes('render()')` - yes, it includes `render()`. And `handlerBody.includes('li.')` - does it include `li.`? Let me check... `todoList.querySelector` - this contains `li.` no... wait, `todoList` - does it contain `li.`? No, `todoList` is one word, not `li.` followed by something. Let me check: `todoList` - the substring `li.` is not found because `todoList` has `li` but not `li.`. 

Wait, what about `editInput`? No, that doesn't contain `li.`.

Hmm, but the assertion says it DOES match. Let me think again...

Actually, `todoList.querySelector('li.editing .edit')` - let me check if this contains `li.`... The string is `todoList.querySelector('li.editing .edit')`. The characters are: `t-o-d-o-L-i-s-t-.-q-u-e-r-y-S-e-l-e-c-t-o-r-('-l-i-.-e-d-i-t-i-n-g- -.-e-d-i-t-')`. 

So `li.` appears in the selector string `'li.editing .edit'` - the part `li.` is right there in the string. So the captured handler body includes `li.` in the `querySelector` argument.

Yes, that's the problem! The conformance check does a simple string match for `li.` in the handler body, and our query selector string `'li.editing .edit'` contains `li.`.

I need to change the query to avoid using the string `li.` in the query selector. Instead of `todoList.querySelector('li.editing .edit')`, I could use `.editing > .edit` or find the editing li differently.

Actually, the simplest fix: since the `li` variable is a DOM element, and the check is for `li.` (variable access), I can use a different approach to find the edit input. Let me use a selector that doesn't contain `li.` as a string:

```js
var editInput = todoList.querySelector('.editing .edit');
```

Or even more specifically:
```js
var editInput = todoList.querySelector('.editing input.edit');
```

But wait, the second Then step says:
```js
Then('the dblclick handler queries the document for the new {string} input after render', function (selector) {
  // The dblclick handler should re-query the DOM for the edit input after render
  // It should use document.querySelector or todoList.querySelector instead of li.querySelector
  const source = this.appSource;
  const dblclickMatch = source.match(/addEventListener\(['"]dblclick['"][^)]*\)\s*\{([^}]*)\}/);
  if (dblclickMatch) {
    const handlerBody = dblclickMatch[1];
    if (handlerBody.includes('render()')) {
      const afterRender = handlerBody.split('render()')[1] || '';
      if (afterRender.includes('li.querySelector') || afterRender.includes('li[')) {
        assert.fail('dblclick handler uses li.querySelector after render() — must re-query the document');
      }
      const hasQuery = /querySelector/.test(afterRender) || /querySelectorAll/.test(afterRender);
      if (!hasQuery) {
        assert.fail('dblclick handler does not query the DOM for the edit input after render()');
      }
    }
  }
});
```

This check looks for `li.querySelector` or `li[` in the part after `render()`. Our code has `todoList.querySelector('li.editing .edit')` which doesn't contain `li.querySelector` or `li[`. So the second check should pass.

But the first check (the one that's failing) looks for `li.` or `li[` ANYWHERE in the handler body (not just after render). And our `'li.editing .edit'` contains `li.` as a substring.

So the fix is to change the query selector to not contain `li.` as a substring. Let me use `.editing .edit` instead:

```js
var editInput = todoList.querySelector('.editing .edit');
```

But wait, I also need to set the `editing` class on the li. The dblclick handler sets `todo.editing = true` and then calls `render()`, which uses `if (todo.editing) li.classList.add('editing')`. So after render(), the li should have the `editing` class. So `.editing .edit` should work.

